#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:1
msgid "Feature Name: `rustdoc_texmath`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:2
msgid "Start Date: 2026-04-30"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:3
msgid ""
"RFC PR: [rust-lang/rfcs#3958](https://github.com/rust-lang/rfcs/pull/3958)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:4
msgid ""
"Rust Issue: [rust-lang/rust#162365](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/162365)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:6
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:9
msgid ""
"Add support for the de facto standard TeX-math-in-markdown syntax to "
"Rustdoc. It’s currently implemented using the [math-core](https://docs.rs/"
"math-core/latest/math_core/) library, which generates MathML Core and is "
"restricted to the subset of LaTeX that can be implemented that way. If you "
"use unsupported syntax, you get a compiler warning from rustdoc."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:13
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:16
msgid ""
"It would be nice if we could write complex equations in our docs. We know "
"that there's demand for this feature, first of all because people have "
"[asked for it](https://internals.rust-lang.org/t/adding-latex-support-to-"
"rustdoc/23858), but mostly because of [crates that did it themselves]"
"(https://github.com/search?q=rustdoc-args+%3D+%5B%22--html-in-header%22%2C+"
"%22katex-header.html%22%2C+%22--cfg%22%2C+"
"%22docsrs%22%5D+language%3Atoml&type=code) by loading [katex.js](https://"
"katex.org/) with inline HTML."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:21
msgid "As far as I know, the most popular ways of doing that are:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:23
msgid ""
"    # Cargo.toml\n"
"    [package.metadata.docs.rs]\n"
"    rustdoc-args = [\"--html-in-header\", \"katex-header.html\", \"--cfg\", "
"\"docsrs\"]\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:29
msgid ""
"    # .cargo/config.toml\n"
"    [build]\n"
"    rustdocflags = [ \"--html-in-header\", \"./src/docs-header.html\" ]\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:33
msgid ""
"Because the first one is only read by docs.rs, and the second one is only "
"read when cargo is run from within the project itself, neither of them work "
"when you use `cargo doc` to browse the docs of your dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:35
msgid ""
"There is a way to make it work in `cargo doc`, but it [seems to be less "
"popular](https://github.com/search?q=%23%21%5Bdoc+"
"%3D+include_str%21%28%22katex.html%22%29%5D+language%3Arust&type=code)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:38
msgid ""
"Providing an easy way to build self-contained docs that work for non-docs.rs "
"readers is the main motivation for adding built-in support for math syntax "
"to Rustdoc, but there are a few other quality of life improvements that come "
"with this feature:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:42
msgid ""
"We can report math syntax errors on the CLI, just like we do for intra-doc "
"links."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:43
msgid ""
"We can render math in the resulting web page without JavaScript. No flash of "
"unstyled content or blocking scripts."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:45
msgid ""
"Built-in TeX math doesn't require double-escaping, because the Markdown "
"parser knows about math, and lets you backslash escape the dollar sign to "
"disable it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:47
msgid "Cross-crate inlining works."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:54
msgid "Guide-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:57
msgid "How to enable"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:59
msgid ""
"To enable `$\\TeX$` math syntax in rustdoc, add this line to your crate root."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:63
msgid ""
"In a future edition, we may enable it by default. If you need to turn it "
"off, add this line to your crate root."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:67
msgid ""
"The only supported params for `doc(syntax)` are `enable(tex_math_dollars)` "
"and `disable(tex_math_dollars)`, but we left the option open for future "
"extensions."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:70
msgid ""
"When this feature is enabled, equations are wrapped in single or double `$` "
"dollar signs."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:74
msgid "The result looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:76
msgid "$$\\sum\\_{i=0}^N x_i$$"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:78
msgid ""
"A detailed comparison between our syntax and KaTeX's can be found [here]"
"(https://tmke8.github.io/math-core/comparison.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:81
msgid "Reference-level explanation"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:84
msgid "Disabling and enabling math syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:86
msgid ""
"The `doc(syntax(enable|disable(tex_math_dollars)))` attribute enables and "
"disables support for parsing `$`\\-delimited TeX math in Rustdoc's Markdown."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:89
msgid ""
"Obviously, you can't set both of them at the same time on a single item. If "
"neither of them are set, rustdoc will use the edition-specific default "
"(which is currently to disable it)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:93
msgid ""
"This attribute can be set on any item that accepts doc comments. The syntax "
"is inherited from the syntactic parent item. So, to list a few examples:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:98
msgid "// lib.rs\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:99
msgid "//! This is *not* math syntax: $x$\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:107 src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:118
#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:130
msgid "/// This *is* math syntax: $x$\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:112 src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:126
msgid "/// This is *not* math syntax: $x$\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:122
msgid ""
"/// <https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/158514#issuecomment-4825510621>\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:137
msgid "// bar.rs\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:138
msgid "//~ ERROR only one doc syntax can be declared on a single item\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:142
msgid ""
"// baz.rs\n"
"//! This *is* math syntax\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:146
msgid "Writing math code in markdown"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:148
msgid ""
"Math expressions are wrapped in `$` signs. One dollar sign means \"inline\" "
"math, and two means \"display\" math."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:151
msgid ""
"Inline math cannot have any whitespace at the start or end of its contents, "
"so `$1$` is a math span, but `$ 1 $` is not. Inline math spans also can't be "
"empty."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:155
msgid ""
"Display math is allowed to have space at the start, so `$$ 1 $$` is a "
"display math span."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:158
msgid ""
"Unescaped curly braces within math spans must balance, and unescaped dollar "
"signs can only appear between unescaped curly braces, so `$$ 1 {$} 2 $$` is "
"parsed as a display math span, but `$$ 1 $ 2 $$` and `$$ { $$` are not."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:163
msgid "Math syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:165
msgid ""
"Within a math span, whitespaces are used for grouping and formatting. But "
"you can't have more than one line break in a row within a math span, because "
"that ends the paragraph that contains it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:169
msgid "Other characters are usually rendered literally, except for"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:171
msgid "backslashes, `\\`, which are the sigil for commands"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:172
msgid "curly braces, `{` and `}`, which are used for command arguments"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:173
msgid "dollar signs, `$`, which delimit math spans"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:174
msgid "number signs, `#`, which are reserved for use in a future macro system"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:175
msgid "ampersands, `&`, which are used for writing matrices and tables"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:176
msgid "circumflex, `^`, which is used for exponents"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:177
msgid "underscore, `_`, which is used for subscript"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:178
msgid "single quote, `'`, which becomes the prime symbol"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:179
msgid ""
"tilde, `~`, which becomes a rendered, non-breaking space (since ordinary "
"spaces are used for grouping)"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:180
msgid "percent, `%`, which mark line comments"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:181
msgid "NUL, which is not allowed"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:183
msgid ""
"Commands are used to write things that can't easily be typed on a keyboard, "
"and for complex layouts like fractions and matrices. The math-core parser "
"that we use implements hundreds of commands."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:187
msgid "Drawbacks"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:190
msgid "There is no such thing as invalid Markdown"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:192
msgid ""
"Adding new syntax to Rustdoc's Markdown is rough, because it's so difficult "
"to do without causing widespread breakage. As spelled out in the [CommonMark "
"spec](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/#characters-and-lines), \"any "
"sequence of characters is a valid CommonMark document,\" so changing "
"anything so that it acts like a metacharacter where it didn't used to "
"changes the behavior of already-valid documents; a _breaking change._"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:200
msgid ""
"And, unlike when GitHub redesigned their Markdown as a CommonMark dialect, "
"we can't run a [one-time batch converter job](https://github.blog/"
"engineering/a-formal-spec-for-github-markdown/#the-migration) over old "
"crates.io crates."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:203
msgid ""
"This class of problem has come up when [intra-doc links were designed]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/54191), when [pulldown-cmark was "
"last updated](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"pull/121659#issuecomment-1992752820), when [hoedown was replaced with "
"pulldown-cmark in the first place](https://internals.rust-lang.org/t/what-to-"
"do-about-pulldown-and-commonmark/5115), and when [anyone proposes replacing "
"Markdown with something else](https://internals.rust-lang.org/t/rustdoc-"
"restructuredtext-vs-markdown/356) that has a \"principled extension\" system."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:216
msgid "Verbosity or breakage as side effect"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:218
msgid ""
"From the perspective of 99% of doc authors who didn't want to write a math "
"span in the first place, false positives that mangle their generated docs "
"are a nasty papercut. Having to read English text without any spaces caused "
"by failing to escape the dollar signs sucks (though not as bad as "
"[accidentally triggering a link refdef](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/133150), as [the result](https://tmke8.github.io/math-core/#input:"
"H4sIAAAAAAAAEwXBwQ0AIQgEwFa2guvCQvaBSoJIhETLv5n2uMIEu6P5MM2JODsF92iVONRBGEseFmsig_79a-7ZDjYAAAA=) "
"might still be legible). The LaTeX math syntax is forgiving enough that "
"English prose is often syntactically valid LaTeX, so Rustdoc invalid LaTeX "
"warnings can't catch every unescaped dollar sign."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:226
msgid ""
"But if we assume that every doc author adds the escapes that they need, this "
"forces doc comments to have more escaped metacharacters than they used to. "
"This makes doc comments less easily readable in their source form, imposing "
"a cost on the 99% that don't want the feature in favor of the 1% who do."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:231
msgid ""
"This argument, if taken to its logical extreme, would imply that we should "
"use plain text doc comments with no extra formatting features. The downside "
"of doing that is similar to the downside of not offering TeX math: users who "
"_really_ want bold text deploy [unicode crimes](https://ux.stackexchange.com/"
"questions/118149/can-screen-readers-interpret-unicode-styles-fonts-such-as-"
"bold-and-italics) and pictures of text, which create accessibility problems."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:240
msgid "Rationale and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:243
msgid "Why LaTeX math in markdown, specifically?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:245
msgid ""
"I would like to avoid the annoying scenario where Rustdoc deploys a "
"complicated, special purpose language, then the community moves on to some "
"new, incompatible language, and we’re stuck maintaining it ourselves because "
"of the stability promise."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:249
msgid ""
"There are a lot of special-purpose technical notations that we might "
"theoretically want to support, but LaTeX-math-in-markdown is special, for "
"two reasons:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:252
msgid ""
"[Lindy effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindy_effect): LaTeX is an "
"established standard that is not going anywhere any time soon."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:253
msgid ""
"There is more than one implementation of the subset of LaTeX that we need."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:255
msgid ""
"The pull request I've been working on uses [math-core](https://docs.rs/math-"
"core/latest/math_core/), but, if that implementation turns out to be "
"problematic, we could pivot to another one, like [pulldown-latex](https://"
"github.com/carloskiki/pulldown-latex), or [katex run in quick-js](https://"
"docs.rs/katex/latest/katex/) [^1]."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:318
msgid "Why use a Rust library, specifically, for the implementation?"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:320
msgid ""
"If your LaTeX syntax doesn't parse, you'll get a warning at Rustdoc compile "
"time, just like you do with broken intra-doc links:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:324
msgid ""
"    error: unknown command \"\\frobnicate\"\n"
"      --> $DIR/basic.rs:6:5\n"
"       |\n"
"    LL | //! $\\frobnicate{2}$\n"
"       |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n"
"       |\n"
"    note: the lint level is defined here\n"
"      --> $DIR/basic.rs:2:9\n"
"       |\n"
"    LL | #![deny(rustdoc::invalid_math)]\n"
"       |         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^\n"
"    \n"
"    error: aborting due to 1 previous error\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:338
msgid ""
"That's harder if the engine is written in a different language than the "
"compiler."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:341
msgid "Why target MathML"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:343
msgid ""
"[MathML Core](https://www.w3.org/TR/mathml-core/) is supported by [most "
"browsers](https://caniuse.com/mathml), but with a [few known bugs](https://"
"github.com/tmke8/math-core/issues/209) that the math-core library tries to "
"work around. It is a strict accessibility improvement over rendering SVG, "
"for example."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:351
msgid "Prior art"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:354
msgid "<https://github.com/cben/mathdown/wiki/math-in-markdown>"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:355
msgid "<https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/LaTeX/Mathematics>"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:356
msgid ""
"The span parsing is based on the [math spec for commonmark-hs](https://"
"github.com/jgm/commonmark-hs/blob/master/commonmark-extensions/test/math."
"md), which is the parser used if you run `pandoc` in `gfm` mode."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:358
msgid ""
"Span parsing is documented in more detail in the [math spec for pulldown-"
"cmark](https://pulldown-cmark.github.io/pulldown-cmark/specs/math.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:363
msgid "Unresolved questions"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:366
msgid "Avoiding Hyrum's Law"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:368
msgid ""
"There are a lot of \\commands in [math-core](https://docs.rs/math-core/"
"latest/math_core/), and some of them are known buggy, meaning they don't "
"match LaTeX itself. We don't want authors to rely on those bugs, either "
"accidentally or in a workaround."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:373
msgid ""
"Normally, we might \"phase in\" new commands by making them unstable first, "
"letting more risk-tolerant authors try it out, then make it available to "
"everyone else. But math-core doesn't have an API for that."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:378
msgid "Font"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:380
msgid "Right now, math formulas default to Noto Sans Math."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:382
msgid ""
"This was chosen because it's inoffensive and fine. But it is a sans serif "
"font face, that will usually be surrounded by serif text."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:384
msgid ""
"We should also look into [subsetting](https://github.com/tmke8/math-"
"core#font-subsetting) the font."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:387
msgid "Future possibilities"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:390
msgid "Extending `doc(syntax)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:392
msgid ""
"The name `tex_math_dollars` is deliberately the same [name and syntax used "
"by Pandoc](https://pandoc.org/MANUAL.html#extension-tex_math_dollars) for "
"this feature. If we add more syntactic features, we can follow the same "
"pattern. An example of how to build on this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:395
msgid ""
"```ebnf\n"
"(* if no language is supplied, the default is \"rustdoc_markdown\", so "
"\"enable(tex_math_dollars)\" is synonymous with \"rustdoc_markdown, "
"enable(tex_math_dollars)\" *)\n"
"doc syntax = [ language ], {\",\", extension list }, [ \",\" ];\n"
"extension list = (\"enable\" | \"disable\"), \"(\", [ extension ], { \",\", "
"extension }, [ \",\" ], \")\" ;\n"
"(* \"commonmark\" enables no extensions (you can, of course, add them)\n"
"  \"rustdoc_markdown\" is synonymous with "
"`commonmark+intra_doc_links+doctests+smart+pipe_tables+strikeout+footnotes+task_lists\"\n"
"  \"gfm\" is synonymous with "
"`commonmark+smart+tex_math_dollars+pipe_tables+strikeout+footnotes+task_lists+emoji+tex_math_gfm+alerts+autolink_bare_uris+yaml_metadata_block\"\n"
"  \"typst\" doesn't support any extensions except \"intra_doc_links\" and "
"\"doctests\", so, for example, `doc(syntax=\"typst+pipe_tables\")` is an "
"error *)\n"
"language = \"commonmark\" | \"rustdoc_markdown\" | \"gfm\" | \"typst\" ;\n"
"extension = \n"
"   \"intra_doc_links\"\n"
" | \"doctests\"\n"
" | \"smart\"\n"
" | \"pipe_tables\"\n"
" | \"strikeout\"\n"
" | \"footnotes\"\n"
" | \"task_lists\"\n"
" | \"tex_math_dollars\"\n"
" | \"emoji\"\n"
" | \"tex_math_gfm\"\n"
" | \"alerts\"\n"
" | \"autolink_bare_uris\"\n"
" | \"yaml_metadata_block\" ;\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:420
msgid ""
"If a parent and child element both have `doc(syntax)` attributes, they "
"aren't merged. The child just overrides the parent."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:422
msgid "Undelimited environments"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:424
msgid ""
"It's a relatively rare feature, but Jupyter Notebook and a few others "
"support LaTeX environments introduced with the `\\begin{foo}` / `\\end{foo}` "
"syntax without wrapping dollar signs. Since backslashes in Markdown only "
"have meaning when followed by punctuation, the false positives shouldn't be "
"that common. Since we don't have to worry about false positives, we can "
"treat it like a CommonMark block construct and allow blank lines in it."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:432
msgid ""
"    /// Computes sum from `start` to `end` of the given function.\n"
"    ///\n"
"    /// \\begin{equation}\n"
"    /// \\sum_{i=start}^{end}{f(i)}\n"
"    /// \\end{equation}\n"
"    fn sum(f: impl FnMut(usize) -> usize, start: usize, end: usize) -> usize "
"{\n"
"        let mut result = 0;\n"
"        for i in start..=end {\n"
"            result += f(i);\n"
"        }\n"
"        result\n"
"    }\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:445
msgid "Custom macros"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:447
msgid ""
"Some implementations, such as [Pandoc's `latex_macros` extension](https://"
"pandoc.org/MANUAL.html#extension-latex_macros), let you write LaTeX macro "
"definitions directly in Markdown. For example, these two doc comments are "
"equivalent:"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:452
msgid ""
"/// \\newcommand{\\tuple}[1]{\\langle #1 \\rangle}\n"
"///\n"
"/// $\\tuple{a, b, c}$\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:458
msgid "/// $\\langle a, b, c \\rangle$\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:463
msgid ""
"Existing users of rustdoc and katex already write custom functions. This "
"isn't a niche feature."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:468
msgid "Drawing and charting syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:470
msgid ""
"There are a lot of different chart formats we _could_ try to support. The "
"tough part is that we want to support it long-term, give error messages at "
"compile time (if the language has a concept of errors), and, ideally, have a "
"specification without much churn."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:475
msgid ""
"[PlantUML](https://github.com/plantuml/plantuml) is pretty much exactly what "
"we would want. But we can't run it (and show error messages) at compile time "
"without bundling a JRE or a JS engine."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:478
msgid ""
"The other obvious choice is [Mermaid](https://github.com/mermaid-js/"
"mermaid), because GitHub supports it. It implements special notations and "
"layout algorithms for commonplace chart notations, so Mermaid syntax is "
"terse and easy to edit. There is also [Merman](https://github.com/Latias94/"
"merman), which implements the same language in Rust. Like Typst, the biggest "
"downside is that they're new and unproven: Mermaid 11.0.0, the most recent "
"breaking change, came out in [August 2024](https://github.com/mermaid-js/"
"mermaid/releases/tag/v11.0.0)."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:484
msgid ""
"[Svgbob](https://github.com/ivanceras/svgbob) actually has a Rust "
"implementation. Ironic, since Svgbob has no syntax errors, it's actually "
"less important to have a Rust implementation than it is for the others, "
"which have the possibility of an \"invalid document\" with errors that we "
"would want to report at compile time. We could fall back to displaying the "
"original ASCII, and the result would still be legible."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:489
msgid ""
"[GraphViz DOT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"DOT_(graph_description_language)) has a Rust [implementation](https://docs."
"rs/layout-rs/latest/layout/), too. Like LaTeX, DOT is popular, stable, and "
"has multiple third-party reimplementations. However, it is _not_ terse "
"unless its layout algorithm does what you need."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:492
msgid ""
"[PIC](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PIC_(markup_language)) is a generic "
"diagram language invented at AT&T Bell Labs in the 80's. The example below "
"was tested in [dpic](https://gitlab.com/aplevich/dpic) and [pikchr](https://"
"pikchr.org/), both maintained implementations of this language."
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:495
msgid "A simple sequence diagram, in each language"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:524
msgid ""
"```graphviz\n"
"digraph SEQ_DIAGRAM {\n"
"    graph [overlap=true, splines=line, nodesep=1.0, ordering=out];\n"
"    node [shape=none, width=0, height=0, label=\"\"];\n"
"\n"
"    // Draw actors\n"
"    {\n"
"        rank=same;\n"
"        node[shape=rectangle, height=0.7, width=2];\n"
"        Actor1 [label=\"Actor1\"];\n"
"        Actor2 [label=\"Actor1\"];\n"
"        Actor3 [label=\"Actor1\"];\n"
"    }\n"
"    // Draw vertical lines\n"
"    {\n"
"        edge [style=dashed, weight=6, arrowhead=none];\n"
"        Actor1 -> a1 -> a2 -> a3;\n"
"        Actor2 -> b1 -> b2 -> b3;\n"
"        Actor3 -> c1 -> c2 -> c3;\n"
"    }\n"
"    // Draws activations\n"
"    { rank=same; a1 -> b1 [label=\"Start\", arrowhead=normal]; b1 -> c1 "
"[style=invis]; }\n"
"    { rank=same; a2 -> b2 [style=invis]; b2 -> c2 [label=\"Do something\", "
"arrowhead=normal]; }\n"
"    { rank=same; a3 -> b3 [arrowhead=normal, dir=back, label=\"Return\"]; b3 "
"-> c3; }\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/3958-rustdoc-texmath.md:551
msgid ""
"```pic\n"
"# dpic requires `.PS` here\n"
"# pikchr doesn't\n"
"Actor1: box \"Actor 1\"; move\n"
"Actor2: box \"Actor 2\"; move\n"
"Actor3: box \"Actor 3\"\n"
"\n"
"line dotted from Actor1.s down 3\n"
"line dotted from Actor2.s down 3\n"
"line dotted from Actor3.s down 3\n"
"\n"
"arrow \"Start\" above from Actor1.s - (0, 1) to Actor2.s - (0, 1)\n"
"arrow \"Do something\" above from Actor2.s - (0, 2) to Actor3.s - (0, 2)\n"
"arrow \"Return\" above from Actor3.s - (0, 3) to Actor1.s - (0, 3)\n"
"# dpic requires `.PE` here\n"
"```"
msgstr ""
